---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getProgram, studioLabel } from '../lib/program';
import { WEIGHTS, RUBRIC_PERCENT, EXAM_PASSING } from '../lib/grading';
import { url } from '../lib/paths';

const program = await getProgram();
let week = 1;
const schedule = program
  .filter((b) => b.studio.data.kind !== 'elective')
  .map((b) => {
    const start = week;
    week += b.studio.data.estWeeks;
    return { b, start, end: week - 1 };
  });
const electives = program.filter((b) => b.studio.data.kind === 'elective');
---

<Base title="Syllabus">
  <div class="prose" style="max-width:860px">
    <h1>Syllabus</h1>
    <p>
      Applied AI Engineering is a self-paced program modeled on a professional master's, with one difference: you are graded on what you build.
      It assumes some Python and comfort with math, and no prior machine learning. At 8 to 10 hours a week it takes about {week - 1} weeks.
    </p>

    <h2>Program goals</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Build LLM applications end to end: prompts, structured outputs, tools, retrieval, agents, and deployment.</li>
      <li>Know which models and techniques fit which jobs (Claude, GPT, Gemini, Grok, and open-weight models), and decide with evidence rather than reputation.</li>
      <li>Build checks and balances into every system: validation, evaluations, human review, guardrails, and observability.</li>
      <li>Leave with a portfolio: six studio projects and a capstone, each in its own repo with a write-up.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Schedule</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Weeks</th><th>Course</th><th>Project</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {schedule.map(({ b, start, end }) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{start}–{end}</td>
            <td><a href={url(`/studios/${b.studio.data.id}/`)}>{studioLabel(b.studio.data)}: {b.studio.data.title}</a></td>
            <td>{b.project?.data.title ?? b.studio.data.tagline}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Electives, any time after Studio 1: {electives.map((b, i) => <>{i > 0 && ', '}<a href={url(`/studios/${b.studio.data.id}/`)}>{b.studio.data.title}</a></>)}.</p>

    <h2>How a lesson works</h2>
    <p>
      Every lesson follows the same rhythm, so no lesson is a wall of text: a short read (each section has a <strong>Listen</strong> button for
      narration), a video or audio segment, a hands-on exercise (Python that runs in your browser, or a local lab for anything that needs an API key),
      and an ungraded knowledge check with explanations. Mark the lesson complete when you are done.
    </p>

    <h2 id="grading">Grading</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Knowledge checks</strong> (end of each lesson): ungraded practice.</li>
      <li><strong>Weekly quizzes</strong>: {WEIGHTS.quizzes * 100}% of the studio grade. Mean of your best attempt on each quiz. Retake as often as you like.</li>
      <li><strong>Studio exam</strong>: {WEIGHTS.exam * 100}% of the studio grade. About 20 questions drawn from a larger pool, optional timer. You need {EXAM_PASSING * 100}% to complete the studio.</li>
      <li><strong>Studio project</strong>: {WEIGHTS.project * 100}% of the studio grade, from your self-assessment against the rubric. Levels map to {RUBRIC_PERCENT[1] * 100}%, {RUBRIC_PERCENT[2] * 100}%, {RUBRIC_PERCENT[3] * 100}%, and {RUBRIC_PERCENT[4] * 100}%.</li>
      <li>Multi-select questions earn partial credit: (correct picks minus wrong picks) divided by the number of correct options, floored at zero.</li>
      <li>Letter grades: A 93+, A- 90+, B+ 87+, B 83+, B- 80+, C+ 77+, C 73+, C- 70+, D 60+, F below.</li>
      <li>A studio is <strong>complete</strong> when all lessons and quizzes are done, the exam is passed, and the project is submitted with its required checklist ticked and rubric scored.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Integrity</h2>
    <p>
      This is a static site, so the answers ship with the page. Nothing stops you from looking, and nothing would be gained. Take quizzes and exams closed-book.
      Score your projects honestly, ideally a day after finishing. Use AI assistants freely when building projects (that is the job), and say in each write-up how you used them.
    </p>

    <h2>Sources and licensing</h2>
    <p>
      Lessons are built from open courses (Microsoft, Hugging Face, Anthropic, MIT OpenCourseWare, Maxime Labonne), official documentation, and peer-reviewed or primary sources.
      Copyrighted books and blogs are linked and summarized, never copied. Every lesson lists its sources, and each lesson shows the date its facts were last checked,
      because this field moves fast. Full list: <a href={url('/credits/')}>Credits</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
